.TH TIMBERSH 1 "2026-08-28" "timberfs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
timbersh \- an interactive shell for timberfs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timbersh
.RI [ --cmd " ARGV" ]
.RI [ --hosts " H,H" ]
.RI [ --rc " FILE" ]
.RI [ --ttl " SECS" ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A console for reading timberfs stores, in a grammar borrowed from SQL. It
selects the stores to read with a predicate, searches them, and pages
through the answer.
.PP
⚠
.B EXPERIMENTAL.
The statements it accepts are NOT promised and change without notice. It
exists to be USED \(em a protocol nobody writes a client against is one
whose awkward parts stay theoretical, and every awkward part this has
found became a fix in timberfs. Do not build anything on its grammar.
.PP
It speaks
.BR timberfs\-query\-document (5)
and nothing else: every statement becomes a document handed to a timberfs
on standard input, and every answer is read back the same way. It
implements no part of the store SELECTOR \(em each host resolves its own,
so a predicate means here exactly what it means there.
.SH WHERE THE FLEET IS
A
.B target
is a name and the command that reaches it. The command belongs to the
TARGET rather than to the session, so an
.BR ssh (1)
to one host and a site wrapper taking the host as an argument are one
fleet \(em which one command with a placeholder could not express.
.PP
The document a resolver prints, and the file that holds the same thing:
.PP
.RS
.EX
{"v": "1.0-EXPERIMENTAL",
 "targets": [
   {"name": "mail01", "cmd": ["ssh", "mail01", "timberfs", "query", "--query", "-"]},
   {"name": "web01",  "cmd": ["site-wrapper", "query", "web01"]},
   {"cmd": ["timberfs", "query", "--query", "-"]}
 ]}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B cmd
is a LIST because a command line written as one string would have to be
split again at this end, under rules that are ours to invent and get
wrong. A
.B name
is what the
.B HOST
column and a
.BI timber:// host /
address say; it is a HINT and not identity, so renaming a target, or
changing how it is reached, leaves every written address valid.
.PP
Sources, most explicit first \(em
.B show hosts;
says which won:
.BR \-\-resolver ,
.B \-\-targets
or
.BR \-\-cmd / \-\-hosts
(one of them, not two);
.BR $TIMBERFS_RESOLVER ;
.BR $TIMBERFS_TARGETS ;
.BR $TIMBERFS_CMD / $TIMBERFS_HOSTS ;
.IR ~/.config/timberfs/targets.json ,
.IR /etc/timberfs/targets.json ;
then one local
.B timberfs query --query -
found on
.BR PATH .
.PP
⚠ A resolver that failed is FATAL and an empty fleet is REFUSED. Falling
back would answer a question about one fleet with a different one, and a
session that quietly asks the local machine instead looks exactly like a
fleet that held nothing.
.PP
⚠ A target that FAILED is not fatal. Something in the system being down
must not stop you reading the logs that are available, so a target that
does not answer is named and the rest are still asked. The two failures
differ: the resolver is how you know what the fleet IS, and being wrong
about that makes every later answer describe the wrong thing; one machine
refusing a connection only means its logs are missing from this answer.
.PP
⚠ A target this build cannot reach \(em a future
.B url
in place of
.B cmd
\(em is NAMED, never dropped, for the same reason: a host that was never
asked must not read as a host that had nothing.
.PP
The resolver is asked ONE question, what the fleet is, and gets no
arguments.
.B refresh
re-runs it, because a resolver derives its answer.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-resolver " CMD"
A command that prints the fleet as a target document.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_RESOLVER )
.TP
.BI \-\-targets " FILE"
The same document, from a file.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_TARGETS )
.TP
.BI \-\-cmd " ARGV"
One command reaching every host, with
.B _TIMBERHOST_
substituted per
.BR \-\-hosts .
Default
.BR "timberfs query --query -" .
It is handed the document on stdin, so anything that reaches a timberfs
works \(em a wrapper,
.BR ssh (1),
a container exec.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_CMD )
.TP
.BI \-\-hosts " H,H"
The hosts that command reaches. Their stores present as one set with a
.B HOST
column, each remembering where it lives.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_HOSTS )
.RS
.PP
A read goes to every host at once and is rendered in the order they were
given. There is no order claimed BETWEEN hosts, for the reason a bounded
timberfs answer is
.B order=sequential
(see
.BR timberfs\-records (5)).
A
.B limit
is a total, so a host whose output falls past it did work that is never
shown \(em the price of not paying the latency serially.
.PP
Several hosts with no
.B _TIMBERHOST_
in
.B \-\-cmd
is refused: every host would get the same command, so one forest would be
read N times and every entry returned N times under a different host's
name.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-rc " FILE"
Statements run at startup, default
.IR ~/.timbershrc .
It is RUN, not read \(em a script of statements, the way
.I .psqlrc
is.
.RB ( $TIMBERSH_RC )
.TP
.BI \-\-histfile " FILE"
Where the line history is kept, default
.IR ~/.timbersh\-history .
.RB ( $TIMBERSH_HISTFILE )
.RS
.PP
⚠ Created mode 0600, and created that way rather than widened and then
narrowed \(em between those two calls the file is readable, which is the
window the mode exists to close. A history of queries is a list of the
identifiers somebody went looking for: request ids, customer names,
hostnames. That is production data in a dotfile, and
.BR psql (1)
has the same exposure and the same answer.
.PP
Failing to read or write it never stops a session. A read\-only home, a
missing directory, a file owned by somebody else \(em none of that is a
reason to refuse to open a shell, so it is reported once and the session
runs without history.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-histsize " N"
How many lines to keep, default 2000.
.RB ( $TIMBERSH_HISTSIZE )
.TP
.BI \-\-ttl " SECS"
Expire the cached store list after SECS. Default 0, which never expires
it \(em
.B refresh
re-reads it and says what changed.
.RB ( $TIMBERSH_STORE_TTL )
.TP
.B \-q
Start without printing the help.
.SH HOSTS AT RUNTIME
.B "add host NAME"
asks it from now on;
.B "drop host NAME"
stops. Both take several names, and both re-read the store list at once,
because the cached one is tagged with the host each store came from and
would otherwise answer for a fleet that is no longer the one being asked.
.PP
A host set and a command must agree, and the disagreement is refused in
BOTH directions because each is wrong in the same silent way. Several
hosts with no
.B _TIMBERHOST_
to put them in sends the same command to each, so one forest is read N
times and every entry comes back N times under a different host's name. A
placeholder with no host to fill it runs the command with
.B _TIMBERHOST_
still in it \(em which reaches something, or nothing, and reports
whatever that was.
.PP
Dropping the last host is refused: a shell with none has nothing to ask.
.SH STATEMENTS
.B \(rsd
lists the stores;
.B \(rsd NAME
or
.B \(rsd ID
describes one.
.B show hosts;
says what each host is and whether anything it said went wrong.
.B \(rs?
prints the grammar, which is the authority \(em this page deliberately
does not copy it, because it moves.
.PP
.B view
opens a store as a tape rather than as an answer, IN PROCESS: the loop it
exists for is search, jump, search again, and a separate program would
have to leave the screen on every hop. It is the same viewer
.BR timberview (1)
runs alone, and it leaves you the address of where you stopped.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timberview (1),
.BR timberfs (1),
.BR timberfs\-query\-document (5),
.BR timberfs\-records (5)
